Do NOT commit to this module without permission from a maintainer;
see the MAINTAINERS file for who they are.

===============================================================================

Discussion about gnome-terminal should happen on desktop-devel-list@gnome.org,
gnome-list@gnome.org, or usability@gnome.org, depending on what sort of
discussion it is. Reporting a bug on http://bugzilla.gnome.org is equally fine.

Remember that the terminal emulation is provided by the VTE widget, maintained
as the 'vte' module on the GNOME SVN. The gnome-terminal product in bugzilla
only covers the menubar, toolbar, and command line options, essentially. VTE,
in turn, is responsible for the actual terminal display.

Terminal session restore
========================

Closing the last terminal window saves the open tabs in order, the selected
tab, and each pane's current working directory. Launch Terminal with no
arguments to reopen them as fresh shells. Missing directories fall back to
the home directory. Closing individual tabs removes them from the session.
Terminal output and running commands are not saved.

To reconnect to SSH when reopening a tab, choose Terminal → SSH on Reopen
(also available from the terminal's context menu). Enter an OpenSSH command,
such as `ssh -p 2222 user@host`, and optionally a password. The setting applies
to the selected pane. Close the last window normally to save the session.
Clear the command to disable reconnection, or choose Forget Password to keep
the command and sign in manually.

The session stores the command and a keyring reference, never the password.
Do not put secrets in the command itself. Password autofill requires the
desktop's Secret Service and applies only to standard SSH password
authentication, not interactive challenges or jump hosts. An unknown host
key requires confirmation; a changed host key blocks the connection.
Reopening starts a new SSH session, not the previous remote shell or directory.

CentOS 7 portable build
=======================

Build on CentOS 7 x86_64 with the GTK 3, VTE, and libsecret development
packages and the usual GNOME Terminal build tools. With the build toolchain
enabled, run:

    sh build-portable.sh /path/to/gnome-terminal-centos7-x86_64.run

The .run file needs a CentOS 7 graphical desktop with GTK, GLib (including
gdbus and gio) and dbus-run-session, but no installation or FUSE. Launch it with:

    sh ./gnome-terminal-centos7-x86_64.run

It extracts to a temporary directory and starts the bundled Terminal on a
private session bus, with preferences stored in the separate
gnome_terminal_portable dconf database. Shells, commands and URI handlers
use the launching desktop's session bus and settings instead. Terminal
keeps its own dark theme without overriding the user's GTK_THEME for
other applications.

The launcher extracts to /dev/shm by default, or to TMPDIR if set.
For remote X11 sessions with an inaccessible or missing XDG_RUNTIME_DIR,
the bundle uses a private runtime directory rather than writing to
/run/user. Terminal settings remain in the portable dconf database.

The launcher keeps working host UTF-8 locales and uses a bundled locale
when needed: zhcn.utf-8 becomes zh_CN.UTF-8; other unavailable locales fall
back to en_US.UTF-8. Child commands keep this locale fallback. System
locale settings are not changed.
